  "verbalization": "Gentiside E is linked to ME-180 through a series of molecular interactions involving histone H1.0, which serves as a target for multiple compounds, including both (2R,3S,4S,5R,6S)-2-(hydroxymethyl)-6-[4-[(E)-prop-1-enyl]phenoxy]oxane-3,4,5-triol and methyl (2R,6R)-6-[(5R,7S,10S,13R,14R,15S,17R)-7,15-dihydroxy-4,4,10,13,14-pentamethyl-3,11-dioxo-2,5,6,7,12,15,16,17-octahydro-1H-cyclopenta[a]phenanthren-17-yl]-2-methyl-4-oxoheptanoate. These compounds are all capable of targeting histone H1.0, which in turn is directly linked to ME-180. This suggests that the mechanism of action for Gentiside E may involve modulating histone H1.0, potentially through interactions that influence gene expression or chromatin structure. The presence of multiple pathways converging on histone H1.0 supports the idea that Gentiside E may act by targeting this histone, thereby affecting ME-180. The evidence highlights a recurring mechanism of histone-targeting, which is a key feature of the drug's interaction with ME-180.",
